  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Iowa has $1.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government made 20,702 awards in Iowa so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $76K. Obligations fell 56.5% year over year. Historical annual obligations show variation: FY21 obligations were $2.5B, FY22 obligations were $2.7B, FY23 obligations were $3.4B, FY24 obligations were $3B, and FY25 obligations were $3.6B. The Department of Defense led buying this fiscal year with $996M in obligations, followed by the Department of the Treasury with $201M and the Department of Agriculture with $112M. ROCKWELL COLLINS, INC. and DATA LINK SOLUTIONS LLC are the largest named vendors this fiscal year, with $347M and $281M respectively. Other large vendors include PMX INDUSTRIES, INC. with $138M and AMERICAN ORDNANCE LLC with $91.1M. Other major industry categories on contract in Iowa include Other Communications Equipment Manufacturing at $300M, Copper Rolling, Drawing, Extruding, and Alloying at $138M, and Radio and Television Broadcasting and Wireless Communications Equipment Manufacturing at $126M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have obligations changed over recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY21 obligations were $2.5B. FY22 obligations were $2.7B, FY23 obligations were $3.4B, FY24 obligations were $3B, FY25 obligations were $3.6B, and FY26 obligations are $1.6B to date. Obligations fell 56.5% year over year. The FY26 total is a fiscal year to date figure, not a full-year total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries account for most spending in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $996M, the Department of the Treasury obligated $201M, and the Department of Agriculture obligated $112M. Other significant buying agencies include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $63.4M and the Department of Energy at $44.3M. Other Communications Equipment Manufacturing accounts for $300M in obligations, Copper Rolling, Drawing, Extruding, and Alloying accounts for $138M, and Radio and Television Broadcasting and Wireless Communications Equipment Manufacturing accounts for $126M."
      }
    ]
